  {"question": "According to arXiv 2609.09604, what are the main user objections to Anthropic's August 2026 watermark deployment, and how does Anthropic respond to each?", "answer": "Users objected that the watermark degrades quality, encodes identifying info, is easily removable, and is inescapable; Anthropic claims it is imperceptible, does not degrade quality, adds no hidden info, and survives light editing.", "evidence": [{"page": 1, "quote": "The users' positions cluster around four claims: (Claim 1) the watermark degrades the quality of the output; (Claim 2) the watermark secretly encodes identifying information, enabling surveillance; (Claim 3) the watermark is easily removable and therefore useless; and (Claim 4) the watermark is effectively non-removable and therefore inescapable."}, {"page": 1, "quote": "Anthropic's technical communication makes correspondingly strong assurances in response: (Assurance 1) the watermark is imperceptible to readers; (Assurance 2) it has no practical impact on the quality or content of the output; (Assurance 3) no hidden characters are inserted into the text, and generating watermarked text consumes no additional tokens, the units in which model output is measured and billed; (Assurance 4) the watermark carries no identifying information and cannot be traced to a specific person, organization, or conversation; and (Assurance 5) the watermark survives light editing, although a complete rewrite will remove it [4], [5]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2609.09604, what are the three categories used to classify claims about watermarking, and which claims require new institutions to verify?", "answer": "Claims are classified as (a) publicly testable, (b) requiring vendor cooperation, and (c) unverifiable without new institutions. Traceability claims require new institutions for verification.", "evidence": [{"page": 2, "quote": "Table I sorts each assertion by what it would take to settle it. An assertion is publicly testable, category (a), when publicly available tools and models suffice to produce decisive evidence. It requires vendor cooperation, category (b), when settling it depends on access that only the vendor can grant, such as matched pairs of outputs generated with and without the watermark, or a detector that reports a confidence score rather than a yes/no answer. It requires new institutions, category (c), when no external experiment can settle it and verification depends on bodies that do not yet exist, such as an independent audit of how the secret keys are managed."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2609.09604, what is the difference between distortion-based and distortion-free watermarking schemes?", "answer": "Distortion-based schemes bias the token distribution, causing a measurable quality cost; distortion-free schemes only affect sampling randomness, leaving individual responses unchanged.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "In the first family, the watermark biases the token distribution. At each step, the key partitions the vocabulary into a green list of favored tokens and a red list of the rest, and the logits, the model's raw scores for each token, are raised by a fixed amount for the green tokens before the next token is sampled [7]. ... In the second family, the next-token distribution is left untouched, and the watermark is carried by the randomness used to sample from it. The key fixes the pseudorandom values that decide which of several equally acceptable tokens is drawn, so that any single response is distributed as it would have been without the watermark [8], [18]."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2609.09604, what is the main limitation acknowledged by Anthropic regarding watermark misattribution?", "answer": "Anthropic acknowledges that the watermark records model involvement, so human-written text edited by the model also carries it, risking misattribution.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "Limitation 1 (Anthropic-acknowledged [4], [5]). The mark records model involvement rather than authorship, so human-written text edited by the model also carries it."} ]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2609.09604, what is the measured effect of the SynthID-Text watermark on prose quality compared to changing the sampling seed?", "answer": "The measured effect of the watermark on prose does not exceed the effect of changing the sampling seed.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "On these models, the measured effect of the watermark on prose does not exceed the effect of changing the sampling seed."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2609.09604, what is the main reason that public debate about watermark removability is confused, according to the authors?", "answer": "Mutually exclusive objections circulate because the debate proceeds without shared evidence, leading to both claims of easy removability and permanence.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "That mutually exclusive objections circulate side by side is a sign of a debate proceeding without shared evidence, which is the gap the taxonomy is meant to fill."}]}
]}
